 Commands that differ from vi

  Ctrl-F/Ctrl-B - Move the view and the cursor a page of SCREEN_ROWS-1 lines, with no overlap: the cursor keeps its
  row, the view stops with the last line on the bottom row (or the first on top), and the cursor then goes on to the
  last (or first) line. Vim keeps two lines of the page on screen (fewer on a screen of under 6 rows), counts the page
  in screen rows, puts the cursor on the new top line (Ctrl-F) or bottom line (Ctrl-B), puts the last line on top
  when the page shows it, and does not move at all when the last line is on top (Ctrl-F) or the first (Ctrl-B).
  Doing all that measured about 117 bytes more code.

  u of 2cc or more (and 2S) - Returns to the column the change was typed at, on the first line put back. Vim puts
  the cursor on the second line, at that column if the line is long enough.

  r with count at end of line - If you do 5ra but only 3 characters remain, the editor replaces those 3. Vi would do
  nothing if there aren't enough characters, as the editor does for 5r<Enter>.

  Counts over 255 - h, l, w, b, e, x, X, ~, J, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B and the word operators (dw, cw, yw, ...) take a count
  of 256 or more as 255, so 300l moves 255 columns and 300x deletes 255 characters. Vi takes the whole count (j and k
  do here too).

  J - Replaces each line break with one space and keeps the joined line's leading blanks: "abc" and "  def" join as
  "abc   def". Vim removes the leading blanks and puts one space in their place, none when the joined line is empty or
  starts with ')' or the line ends in a blank, and two after a '.', '!' or '?' (vim's default 'joinspaces'): "abc def".
  J changes the text in place and keeps only the join points for undo; removing the blanks would change the text's
  length and need their bytes kept for undo, about 190 bytes more code.

  J with a count over 129 - Is refused with "Too many lines to join", as the joins are kept for undo in one 256-byte
  page. Vi joins them all.

  r with a count over 255 - Replaces 255 characters (r<Enter> too: 300r<Enter> takes 255 characters into the line
  break), as the replaced characters are kept for undo in one 256-byte page. Vi replaces them all.

  r with a control key - r takes a printable key or Tab, and Enter or Ctrl-J (a line break, as in vi); other keys cancel
  it. Vi accepts other control characters literally.

  Command line

  : and / input - Limited to what fits the status row after the prompt: the screen width less 2 characters, and at
  most 127 characters for a : command or a search pattern; further keys are ignored. Vim wraps a longer command
  line onto more rows and takes any length.

  Long messages - Are cut off one column short of the screen width. Vim wraps them onto more rows and waits for Enter.

  Characters after a range command letter - d and y take one register name after it, with blanks or not, which
  changes nothing (there is one register: :1,5d_ still yanks the lines, and any character but a digit is taken, where
  vim refuses those that name no register, such as '.'), and anything more is "Unknown command". Vim
  takes letters right after the command letter as part of its name (:1,5da and :2,5yx are "E492: Not an editor
  command", while :1,5delete and :2,5yank work), a digit there as a count (:1,5d3 deletes lines 5-7), > and < repeated
  as more shifts (:1,5>>), and says "E488: Trailing characters" for what follows a register name.

  Marks

  J - The marks of the joined lines are unset; vim moves them to the line they join.

  u of a line delete or J - Leaves unset the marks of the lines that dd, :d or J removed; vim puts back every mark
  that was set when the change began, as u does here after a char delete (x, d, c, s, C) and after cc and S.

  :marks - Lists marks a-z with their line and text; vim also shows the column and its special marks. A long list
  shows a screen at a time with "-- More --", as in vim, but there q ends the list and any other key shows the next
  page (vim also takes Enter or j for one more line, b for the previous page and ESC to stop). Each page starts on a
  cleared screen, where vim scrolls. After the list any key returns; vim asks "Press ENTER or type command to
  continue" and runs a command key typed there.

  Not wrong, but worth noting

  ~ - Accepts a count, which matches vim but not traditional vi. This is probably fine — most users expect vim behavior
  now.
